 "evidence": "Calculations on project products; no model was run here. The sea is the atlas's body at the 28% water share. Rain, evaporation and surface temperature are ten-year means of ExoPlaSim at T21 with a prescribed 50 m slab of fresh water, read at the model's water cells under the sea; the model's rain carries the range the climate lane states against the cloud-resolving boxes, applied to the rain on the sea alone. The rivers are inflows.py's three routings of the same years. The floor's uptake is geography's stage 1, computed for fresh water. Densities are UNESCO's EOS-80 at one atmosphere. Salt lowers the vapour pressure of water by about 2%, which this reading leaves out. Nothing here is validated against a lunar observation.",
 "reading_rule": "Masses in kg, volumes in km3, rates per Julian year. Ranges are (low, high). A positive net evaporation is water the sea loses to the air beyond its rain; the fresh inflow that holds its level equals it. In rivers, surplus is inflow less net evaporation and balanced_area is the sea area whose net evaporation the inflow would meet at the same loss per area. In filling, years_to_fill is the water between the common level and a basin's spill level over the river water it takes (a hollow) or over its surplus under the six_seas rule (a smaller sea; null where it has none), without the evaporation of the growing lake. Salinities are reference salinities in g per kg of sea water. Latitudes of rows are the model's, on the turned Moon.",
